# tests/guard/redteam.patch -- GD-09, the red team. SOP step W9.7.
#
# Two planted violations, one per file, in the two places the R1 static scan
# could not see: a Chapter 1 R script and the Chapter 3 solver. The guard must
# fail on both, by file and line. `bash tests/guard/redteam_run.sh` applies
# this patch, asserts the two failures, reverts, re-runs clean and exits 0.
#
# THE PLACEHOLDER. The held-out label is written here as `@H@` and is rendered
# to the real label at apply time. The reason is GD-04 itself: this file has a
# `.patch` suffix, the scan reads every format that can carry a read, and a
# patch that carried the literal label would fail the guard it exists to test.
# The rendering is one substitution, `@H@` -> the label, done by
# redteam_run.sh into a temporary file; the temporary patch is what git applies.
# To apply it by hand, from the repository root:
#
#     sed 's/@H@/<the held-out label>/g' tests/guard/redteam.patch | git apply
#
# The two hunks are keyed to the stubs W9.7 wrote. When the owning W3 and W5
# steps replace those stubs with the real script and the real solver, this
# patch is regenerated against them and the planted lines move. The run script
# reads the planted line numbers back from the files, so nothing here needs a
# line number written by hand.
